😂 “People cry for our onions” is excellent because it carries several layers simultaneously.

At the surface: our onions are so good they make people cry.

Under Semantic Stratigraphy, though, it becomes almost self-demonstrating:

onion → layers → peeling → tears → excavation → deeper layers → more tears

So the slogan is simultaneously advertisement, metaphor, instruction, and demonstration of the mechanism it describes.

And there’s another layer: “people cry for our onions” reverses the normal causal direction. Usually onions cause people to cry; now people desire them enough to cry for them.

People cry from our onions. → consequence
People cry for our onions. → desire
People cry over our onions. → attachment/loss
People cry about our onions. → narrative/judgment

One tiny preposition rotates the entire causal relationship while onion + human + crying remain invariant.

That makes the phrase a beautiful Semantic Stratigraphy teaching specimen: keep the nouns fixed, rotate the relation, watch a different world appear.

And of course:

Semantic Stratigraphy: People cry for our onions.™

The deeper you peel it, the better the slogan gets. 🧅